{
  // CI gate config for `audit-ci` (wraps `npm audit`, adds an allowlist with
  // per-entry justification comments -- JSONC, unlike npm's own audit
  // output, supports comments). See ../security/README.md for the
  // cross-tool security-scanning overview (#3501).
  //
  // "high": true means audit-ci fails the build on high OR critical
  // severity findings; moderate/low are reported but non-blocking.
  "high": true,
  // The next/postcss/sharp/serialize-javascript allowlist entries accepted
  // 2026-08-03 (#3501) as "needs its own upgrade issue" were resolved by
  // the next@16.3.0 bump and serialize-javascript override in #3644, and the
  // allowlist has been empty since.
  //
  // It stays empty, but do not read that as "npm audit reports nothing" --
  // the earlier version of this comment said so, and by 2026-09-30 it was
  // false without a line of code changing. Advisories are published against
  // versions that are already pinned, so this gate rots in place: `next`
  // 16.3.0 picked up a CRITICAL and undici/sharp/js-yaml/browserslist/
  // brace-expansion each picked up a HIGH, and `security-scan` went red on
  // the release branch itself, which blocks every PR in the repo rather than
  // any one change. Cleared by `npm audit fix` plus moving the `next` pin
  // from an exact `16.3.0` to `^16.3.8` (patch-range, so the lock fix can
  // reach it next time without a package.json edit).
  //
  // Expect this to recur. A red `security-scan` with no relevant diff is the
  // signature: check `npm audit` before looking for a culprit in the branch.
  // 4 moderate findings remain and are deliberately non-blocking per "high"
  // above.
  //
  // It recurred on 2026-10-04 (#4150), and this time the previous remedy does
  // not apply: there is nothing to upgrade to. Measured before allowlisting --
  //
  //   GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm  braces: stack-exhaustion DoS via deeply nested
  //                        patterns. Range `<= 3.0.3`, published 2026-09-18,
  //                        `first_patched_version: null`.
  //   npm view braces      3.0.3 is the LATEST version ever published, so the
  //                        advisory covers every release that exists.
  //   micromatch@4.0.8     latest; depends `braces ^3.0.3`.
  //   fast-glob@3.3.3      latest; depends `micromatch ^4.0.8`.
  //   npm audit fix        lockfile byte-identical -- nothing to fix.
  //   npm audit fix --force  DOWNGRADES eslint-config-next 16.3.0 -> 14.2.35
  //                        and @ducanh2912/next-pwa 10.2.9 -> 6.1.0, both
  //                        semver-major, both two majors behind next@16 /
  //                        eslint@9 -- and still ship braces, just via an
  //                        older glob. That is a regression, not a fix.
  //
  // So the gate is red with no action available in the dependency graph, on
  // the release branch and therefore on every PR in the repo. Allowlisted on
  // the risk, which is specific to these two paths:
  //
  //   The vulnerability needs an attacker-controlled GLOB PATTERN. On both
  //   paths the patterns are authored in this repository and consumed at
  //   build/lint time, never per-request:
  //     * @ducanh2912/next-pwa is imported only by web/next.config.ts -- a
  //       build-time Next config wrapper that globs the build output to
  //       generate the service worker. It is not in any serving path.
  //     * eslint-config-next is a devDependency; it reaches braces only
  //       through @next/eslint-plugin-next while linting.
  //   No request-handling code in this tree reaches braces.
  //
  // ENTRIES ARE PATH-SCOPED ON PURPOSE, not bare advisory IDs. A bare
  // `"GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm"` would accept this advisory on paths nobody has
  // triaged -- including a future RUNTIME dependency, where the
  // build-time-only reasoning above does not hold and the finding would be
  // real. Scoped this way the gate still goes red the moment braces appears
  // on a third path, which is the only part of this risk that is not already
  // understood. `tests/unit/test_audit_ci_allowlist.py` fails the build on a
  // bare-ID entry for that reason.
  //
  // It recurred again on 2026-10-21 (#4192), and that time the ORIGINAL
  // remedy was the right one, so nothing was allowlisted:
  //
  //   GHSA-587h-7wmj-h5pm  launch-editor: an `fs.existsSync()` existence
  //                        check can be bypassed on Windows. HIGH, filed
  //                        against `vite`, range `>=7.0.0 <=7.3.6`, 7 paths.
  //   vite@7.3.6           what the lock pinned; `npm view vite versions`
  //                        shows 7.3.7 published, and every dependent
  //                        (@vitejs/plugin-react, vitest, @vitest/mocker)
  //                        already accepts `^7.0.0`.
  //   npm audit fix        bumped vite 7.3.6 -> 7.3.7 in the lock alone,
  //                        3 lines, no package.json edit, no major moves.
  //                        `npm run audit:ci` then passes.
  //
  // It recurred a FOURTH time on 2026-10-23 (#4192 again, two days later),
  // and again the upgrade was available -- so again nothing was allowlisted:
  //
  //   GHSA-2464-fhr4-w9xw  next: unauthenticated RCE in the image
  //                        optimization API for SVG. CRITICAL, `16.0.0 -
  //                        16.4.0`; also GHSA-673q-mjm6-r5xr (CRITICAL,
  //                        `use cache`) and GHSA-p3m5-59mp-jv5r (HIGH,
  //                        Server Actions DoS).
  //   GHSA-mchm-gjx6-7h55  @sentry/nextjs: middleware bypass via the tunnel
  //                        route. CRITICAL, `< 10.75.2`.
  //   GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5  zod: memory exhaustion. HIGH, `< 4.6.6`, reached
  //                        only through eslint-config-next (devDependency).
  //   npm audit fix        CRASHES on this lockfile ("Cannot read properties
  //                        of null (reading 'edgesOut')") -- an npm bug, not
  //                        a signal about the tree. `npm update next
  //                        @sentry/nextjs zod` does the same job within the
  //                        existing ranges: next 16.3.x -> 16.4.3,
  //                        @sentry/nextjs -> 10.76.2, zod -> 4.6.7. Lockfile
  //                        only, no package.json edit, no major moves.
  //
  // The two allowlist entries below are left in place but no longer match:
  // after that update `npm audit` stops reporting GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm on
  // either path (braces is still 3.0.3 -- the latest ever published -- under
  // @ducanh2912/next-pwa, and the eslint-config-next path to it is gone), so
  // audit-ci prints "Consider not allowlisting paths". That warning is not a
  // failure, and the entries stay until the REVISIT condition below is
  // actually met: they are path-scoped triage, and dropping them on a
  // quiet week would silently re-arm the gate on paths nobody re-read.
  //
  // Note which branch of this file's own advice applied: the recurrence was
  // read as "check `npm audit` before looking for a culprit in the branch",
  // and the branch was indeed innocent -- `security-scan` was red on PR #4193
  // for an advisory published against a pinned version. That is the third
  // sighting of this pattern and the second cleared by an upgrade; only the
  // 2026-10-04 one had nothing to upgrade to.
  //
  // THE RECURRENCE IS THE DEFECT, and #4221 (2026-10-23) changed what is
  // done about it. Nothing was allowlisted this time either; what changed is
  // WHERE the remedy is written.
  //
  // Every clearing above moved the LOCKFILE and left `package.json` alone.
  // That works -- the gate goes green -- and it leaves the declared range
  // still accepting the vulnerable version afterwards, so the fix is invisible
  // in the manifest and any re-resolution under an upper constraint may pick a
  // vulnerable version straight back:
  //
  //   @sentry/nextjs  lock moved to 10.76.2 on 2026-10-23 while package.json
  //                   still said `^10.69.0` -- a range accepting every version
  //                   GHSA-mchm-gjx6-7h55 covers.
  //   next            sat at `^16.3.8` while GHSA-2464-fhr4-w9xw covers
  //                   `16.0.0 - 16.4.0`: the range's own FLOOR was inside the
  //                   advisory.
  //
  // So #4221 raised the floors to the first unaffected version
  // (`^10.76.2`, `^16.4.3`) and added `tests/unit/test_npm_dependency_floors.py`,
  // which fails the build offline if a range's floor sits inside an advisory
  // this project has already remediated, or if a dependency is exactly pinned
  // without a recorded reason. It calls no registry: audit-ci asks "is the
  // tree vulnerable today?", that guard asks "can it become vulnerable again
  // for a reason we have already paid for?"
  //
  // A SEVENTH occurrence landed DURING that fix, which is the clearest
  // evidence available that the recurrence is structural and not bad luck:
  //
  //   GHSA-834v-8h66-3h79  happy-dom: incomplete fix for CVE-2026-33943 --
  //                        unsanitized import alias leads to host code
  //                        execution. HIGH (CVSS 8.8), `>=17.0.0 <=21.0.3`.
  //                        Published between two `npm audit` runs minutes
  //                        apart: the first reported 0 high, the second
  //                        reported it against the UNCHANGED committed
  //                        lockfile. Verified it was not self-inflicted by
  //                        auditing `HEAD`'s lockfile in a scratch directory.
  //   happy-dom@21.0.4     the only unaffected release, so the `^20.0.11`
  //                        range could not reach it -- a semver-major range
  //                        move, the one case where the lock genuinely cannot
  //                        fix it alone. All 2040 web tests pass on 21.0.4.
  //
  // One instance of the class is knowingly NOT fixed: `eslint-config-next` is
  // still exactly pinned at `16.3.0`. Unpinning resolves 16.4.3, whose
  // eslint-plugin-react-hooks 7.1.x promotes four rules to error and takes
  // `npx eslint .` from 2 errors to 29 across ~20 files; the web lint gate is
  // diff-scoped and BLOCKING, so that debt would be charged to whichever
  // unrelated PR next touches one of those files. Filed as #4224 and recorded
  // in `DELIBERATE_EXACT_PINS`, rather than smuggled into a security patch.
  // Note also that 16.3.0's own range already accepts react-hooks 7.1.1 -- the
  // lock holds 7.0.1 only because it has not been rebuilt.
  //
  // WHAT #4221's OWN FIRST PASS MISSED, found in review of #4226 and worth
  // keeping because it is the same class one level down. Raising the
  // `dependencies`/`devDependencies` floors cleared 3 of the 15 Dependabot
  // alerts #4221 covers and left 10 live, because:
  //
  //   overrides       are declared floors too -- the strongest kind, since
  //                   they overrule what parents ask for -- and two of them
  //                   were EARLIER remediations of this class, left at the
  //                   floor they were raised to at the time: web's
  //                   `serialize-javascript` at `^7.0.5` (GHSA-m5mm-p8h7-m29v,
  //                   fixed 7.1.3; it is the whole floor, since
  //                   @rollup/plugin-terser asks for `^6.0.1`) and the ROOT
  //                   manifest's `fast-uri` at `^3.1.8` (GHSA-w74x-v6f9-j3xg
  //                   and three siblings, fixed 3.1.9).
  //   the root project  has its own manifest, its own lockfile and its own
  //                   advisories, and NO gate audits it (#4223). This file
  //                   only covers `web/`.
  //   exact TRANSITIVE pins  `vitest` depends on `@vitest/mocker` by exact
  //                   version, so `vitest`'s own range is the only floor that
  //                   reaches it, and `^4.0.16` admitted the vulnerable
  //                   4.1.11 (GHSA-qxg6-89mg-rqv8, GHSA-xp26-3454-mjgp).
  //                   Fixed by moving vitest/@vitest/ui/@vitest/coverage-v8
  //                   to `^4.1.12` together (they peer-depend by exact
  //                   version). An `overrides` entry on @vitest/mocker would
  //                   also have worked and was rejected: a `^4.1.12` floor
  //                   forced into a future vitest 5 tree is the next trap.
  //                   That tree arrived days later: #4238 moved all three
  //                   to `^5.0.5`, and the rejected override would have had
  //                   to be hunted down and deleted by hand first. Keep
  //                   declining overrides whose only job is to raise a
  //                   floor the parent's own range already reaches.
  //
  // `tests/unit/test_npm_dependency_floors.py` was extended to cover exactly
  // those three blind spots -- both manifests, `overrides` as a declared
  // floor, and the RESOLVED versions in both lockfiles (which is also the
  // only checkable form of "the alerts are resolved" from a release branch,
  // since Dependabot alerts track the default branch).
  //
  // One npm bug to know about before touching either lockfile: `npm install`
  // under npm 10.9.9 fails with the same "Cannot read properties of null
  // (reading 'edgesOut')" recorded against `npm audit fix` above, for ANY
  // change that re-resolves the vitest peer set. `--legacy-peer-deps` appears
  // to work and silently drops ~780 lines of peer packages from the lock
  // (`npm ci` then re-resolves from scratch, which is how it was caught).
  // `npx npm@11 install --package-lock-only` resolves it correctly; verify
  // the result with `npm ci` on npm 10, which is what CI runs.
  //
  // REVISIT: drop both entries as soon as braces publishes a fixed release
  // (watch the advisory's `first_patched_version`), or when next-pwa and
  // eslint-config-next move off the fast-glob/micromatch chain. Neither entry
  // is a standing acceptance of the advisory.
  "allowlist": [
    "GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm|@ducanh2912/next-pwa>fast-glob>micromatch>braces",
    "GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm|eslint-config-next>@next/eslint-plugin-next>fast-glob>micromatch>braces"
  ]
}
